            "content": "solution.\n\n**### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have proved that for \\(n=3\\) there is **no** configuration of three distinct lines in the plane that covers all required points and contains exactly \\(k=2\\) sunny lines. Hence the requested construction is impossible.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. List all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\) and \\(a+b\\le 4\\); there are six points.  \n2. Observe that a *sunny* line cannot be vertical, horizontal, or have slope \\(-1\\).  \n3. Determine all possible *non‑sunny* lines (vertical, horizontal, or slope \\(-1\\)) that pass through the six points; each such line contains either 1, 2, or 3 of the points.  \n4. Since each sunny line can contain at most two of the six points (a direct check of all pairs shows that no three are collinear with a sunny slope), the non‑sunny line must contain at least two points.  \n5. Exhaustively examine every non‑sunny line that contains 2 or 3 points, and for each case show that the remaining points cannot be covered by two sunny lines (either because the remaining set contains no sunny pair, or because it would require a third line).  \n6. Conclude that no choice of the single non‑sunny line leads to a feasible covering, so a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does not exist.  \n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=3\\),\n\n\\[\nS_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), undefined (vertical), and \\(-1\\).  \nWe must use three distinct lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) such that  \n\n* every point of \\(S_3\\) lies on at least one of the three lines, and  \n* exactly two of the lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  How many points can a sunny line contain?  \n\nFor each unordered pair of points of \\(S_3\\) compute the slope:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{pair} & \\text{slope}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1)-(1,2) & \\text{vertical}\\\\\n(1,1)-(1,3) & \\text{vertical}\\\\\n(1,1)-(2,1) & 0\\\\\n(1,1)-(2,2) & 1\\\\\n(1,1)-(3,1) & 0\\\\\n(1,2)-(1,3) & \\text{vertical}\\\\\n(1,2)-(2,1) & -1\\\\\n(1,2)-(2,2) & 0\\\\\n(1,2)-(3,1) & -\\tfrac12\\\\\n(1,3)-(2,1) & -2\\\\\n(1,3)-(2,2) & -1\\\\\n(1,3)-(3,1) & -1\\\\\n(2,1)-(2,2) & \\text{vertical}\\\\\n(2,1)-(3,1) & 0\\\\\n(2,2)-(3,1) & -1\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThe only slopes that are **not** \\(0\\), undefined, or \\(-1\\) are \\(1\\), \\(-\\tfrac12\\), and \\(-2\\).  \nHence a sunny line can contain at most **two** points of \\(S_3\\); no three points of \\(S_3\\) are collinear with a sunny slope.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Possible non‑sunny lines  \n\nA non‑sunny line must be vertical (\\(x=c\\)), horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), or have slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  \nIntersecting these families with the set (1) gives:\n\n| type | equation | points of \\(S_3\\) on the line | cardinality |\n|------|-----------|------------------------------|--------------|\n| vertical | \\(x=1\\) | \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) | 3 |\n| vertical | \\(x=2\\) | \\((2,1),(2,2)\\) | 2 |\n| vertical | \\(x=3\\) | \\((3,1)\\) | 1 |\n| horizontal | \\(y=1\\) | \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\) | 3 |\n| horizontal | \\(y=2\\) | \\((1,2),(2,2)\\) | 2 |\n| horizontal | \\(y=3\\) | \\((1,3)\\) | 1 |\n| diagonal | \\(x+y=2\\) | \\((1,1)\\) | 1 |\n| diagonal | \\(x+y=3\\) | \\((1,2),(2,1)\\) | 2 |\n| diagonal | \\(x+y=4\\) | \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\) | 3 |\n\nSince each sunny line can cover at most two points, the single non‑sunny line must contain **at least two** points; otherwise the two sunny lines could cover at most four points, leaving uncovered points.  \nThus we only need to consider the non‑sunny lines that contain 2 or 3 points, i.e. the six lines listed in the table with cardinality \\(2\\) or \\(3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Exhaustive check  \n\nFor each candidate non‑sunny line \\(N\\) we examine the set \\(R=S_3\\setminus N\\) of points that must be covered by the two sunny lines.  \nBecause a sunny line can contain at most two points, the only way to cover \\(R\\) with two sunny lines is to partition \\(R\\) into two (possibly singleton) subsets, each of which is collinear with a sunny slope.\n\nBelow we list \\(R\\) and all possible sunny pairs inside \\(R\\).\n\n| \\(N\\) | points on \\(N\\) | \\(R\\) (remaining points) | sunny pairs in \\(R\\) |\n|------|----------------|--------------------------|----------------------|\n| \\(x=1\\) | \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) | \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\) | none |\n| \\(y=1\\) | \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\) | \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\) | none |\n| \\(x+y=4\\) | \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\) | \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) | none |\n| \\(x=2\\) | \\((2,1),(2,2)\\) | \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\) | only \\((1,2),(3,1)\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)) |\n| \\(y=2\\) | \\((1,2),(2,2)\\) | \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\) | only \\((1,3),(2,1)\\) (slope \\(-2\\)) |\n| \\(x+y=3\\) | \\((1,2),(2,1)\\) | \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\) | only \\((1,1),(2,2)\\) (slope \\(1\\)) |\n\n*For the three lines of size 3* (\\(x=1\\), \\(y=1\\), \\(x+y=4\\)): the remaining three points contain **no** sunny pair; consequently at least one of the two sunny lines would have to contain a single required point, leaving the fourth point uncovered. Hence these cases are impossible.\n\n*For the three lines of size 2*: each remaining set \\(R\\) contains **exactly one** sunny pair, leaving two points that are either vertically, horizontally, or diagonally aligned (slopes \\(0\\), undefined, or \\(-1\\)). Those two points cannot be placed on a sunny line, so again a covering with only two sunny lines is impossible.\n\nThus **every** possible choice of the single non‑sunny line leads to a contradiction.\n\n----------------------------------