ine. Hence the two sunny lines cannot cover \\(R\\).\n\n6. Since **every** possible choice of the single non\u2011sunny line leads to a contradiction, a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does not exist.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{No configuration exists for }k=2\\text{ when }n=3.}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_3=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le4\\}\n      =\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope \\(\\neq0\\), \\(\\neq\\) undefined (vertical), and \\(\\neq-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Sunny lines contain at most two points of \\(S_3\\)\n\nFor each unordered pair of points of \\(S_3\\) we compute the slope:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{pair} & \\text{slope}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1)-(1,2) & \\text{vertical}\\\\\n(1,1)-(1,3) & \\text{vertical}\\\\\n(1,1)-(2,1) & 0\\\\\n(1,1)-(2,2) & 1\\\\\n(1,1)-(3,1) & 0\\\\\n(1,2)-(1,3) & \\text{vertical}\\\\\n(1,2)-(2,1) & -1\\\\\n(1,2)-(2,2) & 0\\\\\n(1,2)-(3,1) & -\\tfrac12\\\\\n(1,3)-(2,1) & -2\\\\\n(1,3)-(2,2) & -1\\\\\n(1,3)-(3,1) & -1\\\\\n(2,1)-(2,2) & \\text{vertical}\\\\\n(2,1)-(3,1) & 0\\\\\n(2,2)-(3,1) & -1\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThe only slopes that are **not** \\(0\\), undefined, or \\(-1\\) are \\(1\\), \\(-\\tfrac12\\), and \\(-2\\).  \nNo three points of \\(S_3\\) share any of these three slopes; therefore a sunny line can contain at most **two** points of \\(S_3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Non\u2011sunny lines that contain at least two points\n\nA non\u2011sunny line must be of one of the forms  \n\n\\[\nx=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nIntersecting each family with \\(S_3\\) gives:\n\n| type | equation | points of \\(S_3\\) on the line | cardinality |\n|------|----------|------------------------------|--------------|\n| vertical | \\(x=1\\) | \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) | 3 |\n| vertical | \\(x=2\\) | \\((2,1),(2,2)\\) | 2 |\n| horizontal | \\(y=1\\) | \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\) | 3 |\n| horizontal | \\(y=2\\) | \\((1,2),(2,2)\\) | 2 |\n| diagonal | \\(x+y=3\\) | \\((1,2),(2,1)\\) | 2 |\n| diagonal | \\(x+y=4\\) | \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\) | 3 |\n\nAll other non\u2011sunny lines contain at most one point of \\(S_3\\) and are therefore irrelevant, because the two sunny lines together can cover at most four points.\n\nThus the only candidates for the **single** non\u2011sunny line are the six lines listed above.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Exhaustive analysis of each candidate\n\nFor each candidate non\u2011sunny line \\(N\\) we set \\(R=S_3\\setminus N\\) and ask whether the two sunny lines can cover \\(R\\).\n\n---\n\n**(i) \\(|N|=3\\).**  \n\n* \\(N:x=1\\) \u2192 \\(R=\\{(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  \n  Slopes among points of \\(R\\): vertical, horizontal, and \\(-1\\); none is sunny.  \n* \\(N:y=1\\) \u2192 \\(R=\\{(1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\}\\).  \n  Again all pairwise slopes are vertical, horizontal, or \\(-1\\).  \n* \\(N:x+y=4\\) \u2192 \\(R=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \n  Pairwise slopes are vertical, horizontal, or \\(-1\\).\n\nIn each case \\(R\\) contains three points but **no** sunny pair, so two sunny lines cannot cover all of \\(R\\).\n\n---\n\n**(ii) \\(|N|=2\\).**  \n\n* \\(N:x=2\\) \u2192 \\(R=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\}\\).  \n  The only sunny pair in \\(R\\) is \\((1,2),(3,1)\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).  \n  The remaining points \\((1,1),(1,3)\\) are vertical, a non\u2011sunny direction; they cannot lie on a sunny line.  \n\n* \\(N:y=2\\) \u2192 \\(R=\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\}\\).  \n  The only sunny pair is \\((1,3),(2,1)\\) (slope \\(-2\\)).  \n  The leftover points \\((1,1),(3,1)\\) are horizontal, non\u2011sunny.  \n\n* \\(N:x+y=3\\) \u2192 \\(R=\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  \n  The only sunny pair is \\((1,1),(2,2)\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  \n  The remaining points \\((1,3),(3,1)\\) lie on the line \\(x+y=4\\) (slope \\(-1\\)), which is non\u2011sunny.\n\nThus in every \\(|N|=2\\) case the set \\(R\\) cannot be covered by two sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion\n\nAll possible choices for the unique non\u2011sunny line lead to a contradiction. Consequently there is **no** arrangement of three distinct lines that covers every point of \\(S_3\\) while having exactly two sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{No configuration exists for }k=2\\text{ when }n=3.}\n\\]"
