over every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n2. **Sunny part.**  \n   The points that are still uncovered are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(a',b')\\mid a',b'\\ge1,\\;a'+b'\\le k+1\\}=T_k .\n   \\]\n\n   By Lemma 1 there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\).\n\nThe union of the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines and the \\(k\\) sunny lines gives a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines. Hence a configuration exists for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume, to the contrary, that for some \\(n\\ge3\\) there is a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with exactly two sunny lines.\n\nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,1),\\qquad P_2=(1,2),\\qquad P_3=(2,1).\n\\]\n\nAny line through two of them has slope \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=3\\)); therefore **no sunny line can contain more than one** of the three points.  \nConsequently at least two of the points must be covered by non‑sunny lines.  \nThe only non‑sunny lines that can pass through two of them are \\(x=1\\) (covers \\(P_1,P_2\\)) and \\(y=1\\) (covers \\(P_1,P_3\\)).  \nThus both \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) belong to \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\nDelete these two lines from \\(\\mathcal F\\).  \nThe remaining uncovered points are  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}=T_{\\,n-2}.\n\\]\n\nThe remaining family \\(\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{x=1,y=1\\}\\) consists of the two sunny lines and possibly further non‑sunny lines, but its total size is \\(n-2\\).\n\n*If \\(n\\ge4\\)* then \\(|T_{\\,n-2}|\\ge3\\).  \nLemma 1 tells us that covering a triangular set of size at least three requires at least three sunny lines; with only two sunny lines available the set \\(T_{\\,n-2}\\) cannot be completely covered. This contradicts the assumption.\n\n*If \\(n=3\\)* then \\(S=\\{(1,2),(2,1),(2,2)\\}\\).  \nAgain, any two of these three points lie on a forbidden line, so two sunny lines cannot cover all three. Hence the assumption also fails for \\(n=3\\).\n\nThus no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nAll admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. every integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) except \\(k=2\\). ∎"
