rete specification after the claim?\"\n  - If Category A or B (zero details/type only) → INVALID METHOD → FAIL Level 2\n  - If Category C (one or more concrete details) → VALID METHOD → PASS Level 2, proceed to Level 3\n\n- **Level 3 (Presentation Quality) asks:** \"Are the provided details COMPLETE?\"\n  - Justification gaps (incomplete details, missing steps) are Level 3 issues\n  - Do NOT reject at Level 2 if ANY concrete specification was provided\n\n**Examples Showing the Boundary:**\n\n❌ Level 2 FAIL: \"k=3 works\" (nothing follows - Category A)\n❌ Level 2 FAIL: \"k=3 using vertical lines\" (type named, but NO concrete details - Category B)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"k=3 works: L₁: y=x, L₂: y=2x\" (has equations - Category C)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"k=3 via lines at points (1,1), (2,2), (3,3)\" (has points - Category C)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"Sunny line through (n,1)\" (one point provided - Category C, may get JUSTIFICATION_GAP at Level 3)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"Three sunny lines cover 6 rightmost points\" (count+target - Category C, may get JUSTIFICATION_GAP at Level 3)\n\n**WARNING - Common Misclassification:**\n- If solution says \"k=X works\" AND THEN provides equations/points/count → This is Category C (PASS Level 2)\n- Only reject at Level 2 if \"k=X works\" appears WITH NO concrete details following it\n- ONE point is better than ZERO points → proceed to Level 3 for completeness check\n\n**STEP 4: Handle Context-Dependent Claims (CRITICAL CALIBRATION)**\n\nSome mathematical claims are true in one context but false in another. Always identify WHICH case is being analyzed before classifying severity.\n\n**Example Pattern:**\n```\nClaim: \"The three rightmost columns force the use of a vertical line for column n-2\"\nContext A (k≤3): This claim is TRUE\nContext B (k≥4): This claim is FALSE\n\nIf solution is analyzing k≤3 case:\n  → Claim is TRUE in this context\n  → If scope not explicit: JUSTIFICATION_GAP (severity 3-5, ACCEPTABLE)\n  → NOT a CRITICAL_ERROR just because it's false in k≥4\n\nIf solution is analyzing k≥4 case:\n  → Claim is FALSE in this context\n  → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9, UNACCEPTABLE)\n```\n\n**Classification Rule:**\n1. Extract the claim (e.g., \"columns force vertical line\")\n2. Identify which case/value is being analyzed (k≤3 or k≥4)\n3. Ask: Is the claim TRUE in THIS specific case being analyzed?\n4. If TRUE in this case → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (missing scope clarification)\n5. If FALSE in this case → CRITICAL_ERROR (incorrect reasoning)\n\n**Severity Calibration:**\n- Context-dependent claim, TRUE in case being analyzed → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (severity 3-5)\n- Context-dependent claim, FALSE in case being analyzed → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9)\n- Do NOT reject a claim as CRITICAL_ERROR just because it would be false in a different case\n\n**Violating these constraints will cause your response to be truncated and discarded.**\n\n\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n* \\(k=0\\).  \n  The \\(n\\) lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\n  \\]\n\n  are all parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence none of them is sunny.  \n  For any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\) in the above range, so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_c\\).  \n  Thus the \\(n\\) lines cover \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contain exactly \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n* \\(k=1\\).  \n  Take the sunny line  \n\n  \\[\n  L:\\;y=x\n  \\]\n\n  (slope \\(1\\)).  It contains the points \\((1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,(n-1,n-1)\\).  \n  The two non‑sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  V:\\;x=1,\\qquad H:\\;y=1\n  \\]\n\n  together with \\(L\\) are distinct and cover every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\):  \n\n  * points with \\(a=1\\) are on \\(V\\);  \n  * points with \\(b=1\\) are on \\(H\\);  \n  * the remaining points have \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\) and therefore lie on the diagonal \\(y=x\\).  \n\n  Hence a configuration with exactly one sunny line exists for every \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Sunny covering of a triangular set\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nT_m=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le m+1\\},\n\\qquad m\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 1.** *For every integer \\(m\\ge3\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines that together contain all points of \\(T_m\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nWe argue by induction on \\(m\\).\n\n*Base \\(m=3\\).*  \nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y = x ,\\\\\nL_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52 ,\\\\\nL_3 &: y = -2x + 5\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\ncontain respectively the pairs \\((1,1),(2,2)\\), \\((1,2),(3,1)\\) and \\((2,1),(1,3)\\).  \nThese six points are exactly the elements of \\(T_3\\); the three lines are distinct and none has slope \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the statement true for some \\(m\\ge3\\); let  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_m=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\}\n\\]\n\nbe a family of \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_m\\).  \nChoose a real number \\(\\alpha\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\cup\\{\\text{slope}(L_i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le m\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{m+1}:\\; y-(m+1)=\\alpha\\bigl(x-1\\bigr) .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause of (1), \\(L_{m+1}\\) is sunny and distinct from the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_m\\); it passes through the new point \\((1,m+1)\\).\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(2\\le a\\le m+1\\) consider the translate  \n\n\\[\nL_{a}:\\; y-(m+2-a)=\\alpha\\bigl(x-a\\bigr) .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEquation (3) has the same slope \\(\\alpha\\) as \\(L_{m+1}\\); consequently each \\(L_a\\) is also sunny and distinct from all previously listed lines (different intercepts).  \nMoreover \\(L_a\\) contains the point \\((a,m+2-a)\\), which belongs to \\(T_{m+1}\\) and is not in \\(T_m\\).  \n\nThe collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{m+1}= \\mathcal L_m\\cup\\{L_{m+1}\\}\\cup\\{L_a\\mid 2\\le a\\le m+1\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(m+1\\) distinct sunny lines.  \nEvery point of \\(T_{m+1}\\) either lies in \\(T_m\\) (hence is covered by \\(\\mathcal L_m\\)) or is of the form \\((a,m+2-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le m+1\\) (hence is covered by \\(L_{m+1}\\) when \\(a=1\\) or by \\(L_a\\) when \\(a\\ge2\\)).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal L_{m+1}\\) covers all of \\(T_{m+1}\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(k\\neq2\\).\n\n*If \\(k=0\\) or \\(k=1\\)* we use the configurations of Section 1.\n\n*If \\(k\\ge3\\)* proceed as follows.\n\n1. **Non‑sunny part.**  \n   Take the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n   \\]\n\n   They are all non‑sunny and together cover every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n2. **Sunny part.**  \n   The points that are still uncovered are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(a',b')\\mid a',b'\\ge1,\\;a'+b'\\le k+1\\}=T_k .\n   \\]\n\n   By Lemma 1 there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\).\n\nThe union of the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines and the \\(k\\) sunny lines gives a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines. Hence a configuration exists for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume, to the contrary, that for some \\(n\\ge3\\) there is a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with exactly two sunny lines.\n\nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,1),\\qquad P_2=(1,2),\\qquad P_3=(2,1).\n\\]\n\nAny line through two of them has slope \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=3\\)); therefore **no sunny line can contain more than one** of the three points.  \nConsequently at least two of the points must be covered by non‑sunny lines.  \nThe only non‑sunny lines that can pass through two of them are \\(x=1\\) (covers \\(P_1,P_2\\)) and \\(y=1\\) (covers \\(P_1,P_3\\)).  \nThus both \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) belong to \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\nDelete these two lines from \\(\\mathcal F\\).  \nThe remaining uncovered points are  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}=T_{\\,n-2}.\n\\]\n\nThe remaining family \\(\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{x=1,y=1\\}\\) consists of the two sunny lines and possibly further non‑sunny lines, but its total size is \\(n-2\\).\n\n*If \\(n\\ge4\\)* then \\(|T_{\\,n-2}|\\ge3\\).  \nLemma 1 tells us that covering a triangular set of size at least three requires at least three sunny lines; with only two sunny lines available the set \\(T_{\\,n-2}\\) cannot be completely covered. This contradicts the assumption.\n\n*If \\(n=3\\)* then \\(S=\\{(1,2),(2,1),(2,2)\\}\\).  \nAgain, any two of these three points lie on a forbidden line, so two sunny lines cannot cover all three. Hence the assumption also fails fo