nts.  Hence \\(k=2\\) cannot occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nP_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nThus a non‑sunny line is of one of the three forms  \n\n\\[\nx=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c\\qquad(c\\in\\mathbb R).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Diagonal covering)\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) the line  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x+y=c\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly the points of \\(P_n\\) whose coordinates sum to \\(c\\).  \nThe \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{L_c\\mid c=2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) are distinct, none of them is sunny, and together they cover all points of \\(P_n\\). ∎  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Two‑point sunny line)\n\nLet \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in P_n\\) be distinct.  \nIf \\((a_2-a_1)(b_2-b_1)\\neq 0\\) and \\((b_2-b_1)/(a_2-a_1)\\neq -1\\) then the line through the two points has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently it is sunny. ∎  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Pairing)\n\nLet \\(S\\subseteq P_n\\) be a finite set such that no three points of \\(S\\) lie on a line of slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  \nThen the points of \\(S\\) can be partitioned into \\(\\lceil|S|/2\\rceil\\) disjoint unordered pairs, each pair determining a distinct sunny line.  \n\n*Proof.*  Choose any point \\(p\\in S\\).  Because at most two points of \\(S\\) share a forbidden line with \\(p\\), there exists another point \\(q\\in S\\setminus\\{p\\}\\) such that the line \\(pq\\) is sunny (Lemma 2).  Remove \\(\\{p,q\\}\\) and repeat.  The process stops after at most one point remains, which can be taken alone on a sunny line of arbitrary non‑forbidden slope. ∎  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 4 (Impossibility for \\(n=3,\\;k=2\\))\n\nAssume that for \\(n=3\\) three distinct lines cover the six points of \\(P_3\\) and exactly two of them are sunny.  \nHence one line is non‑sunny, i.e. it is of the form \\(x=c\\), \\(y=c\\) or \\(x+y=c\\).  \n\n*Case 1.*  The non‑sunny line is \\(x=c\\) or \\(y=c\\).  \nSuch a line contains at most three points of \\(P_3\\); the remaining three points are not collinear on a sunny line (they form an L‑shape).  Two sunny lines can cover at most four points, so at least one point stays uncovered – contradiction.\n\n*Case 2.*  The non‑sunny line is \\(x+y=c\\).  \nThen it contains exactly the points whose sum equals \\(c\\).  The other four points have two different sums, hence any line through two of them has slope \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\) and is non‑sunny.  Consequently the two sunny lines can each contain at most one of the remaining points, leaving at least two points uncovered – contradiction.\n\nThus no configuration with \\(k=2\\) exists for \\(n=3\\). ∎  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for general \\(n\\) and arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and put \\(t=n-k\\) (the required number of non‑sunny lines).\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 – non‑sunny lines.**  \n\n*If \\(t\\ge 2\\):* take the vertical line \\(x=1\\) and the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \n*If \\(t=1\\):* take the diagonal line \\(x+y=2\\).  \n*If \\(t=0\\):* take none.\n\nAll chosen lines are non‑sunny and distinct.\n\n---\n\n**Step 2 – points still uncovered.**  \n\nDenote by \\(U\\) the set of points of \\(P_n\\) not lying on any line of Step 1.  \nA direct inspection shows  \n\n\\[\n|U|\\le 2k .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, when \\(t\\ge 2\\) the lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) remove every point with \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\); the remaining points satisfy \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  The number of such points equals \\(\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2\\), which is at most \\(2k\\) because \\(k=n-t\\ge n- (n-2)=2\\).  When \\(t=1\\) the single diagonal line removes all points with sum \\(2\\); the remaining points have sums \\(3,\\dots ,n+1\\), i.e. at most \\(2k\\) points because \\(k=n-1\\).  When \\(t=0\\) we have \\(U=P_n\\) and \\(|U|= \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le 2n\\) (true for all \\(n\\ge3\\)).  Hence (1) holds in every case.\n\n---\n\n**Step 3 – sunny lines.**  \n\nApply Lemma 3 to the set \\(U\\).  Because \\(|U|\\le 2k\\), we can partition \\(U\\) into at most \\(k\\) pairs (and possibly one single point).  Each pair determines a sunny line by Lemma 2; a single point can be placed on any sunny line of a non‑forbidden slope.  The lines obtained are distinct because each contains a point of \\(U\\) that does not belong to any other line of the construction.\n\nThus we have produced exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines together with the \\(t=n-k\\) non‑sunny lines of Step 1, all distinct, covering every point of \\(P_n\\).\n\nConsequently, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(n\\ge4\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Completion of the case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe six points of \\(P_3\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1),\\;(1,3),\\;(3,1),\\;(2,2).\n\\]\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the three diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,3,4\\) (Lemma 1).  \n* \\(k=1\\): non‑sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\); the remaining point \\((2,2)\\) is covered by the sunny line \\(y-2=2(x-2)\\).  \n* \\(k=3\\): three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  &\\ell_1:\\;y=x &&\\text{through }(1,1),(2,2),\\\\\n  &\\ell_2:\\;y-3=-\\tfrac12(x-1) &&\\text{through }(1,3),(3,1),\\\\\n  &\\ell_3:\\;y-1=-2(x-2) &&\\text{through }(2,1),(1,3)\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  cover all six points.  \n\n* \\(k=2\\): impossible by Lemma 4.\n\nHence for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(k=0,1,3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion  \n\nAll admissible values of \\(k\\) are\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]  "
