                "content": ".**Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Notation  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) let  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) consists of the lattice points that have to lie on the\nchosen \\(n\\) lines.\nA line is called **sunny** when its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Easy extreme values  \n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The \\(n\\) lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_s:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\n  \\]\n\n  are all parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence none of them is sunny.\n  For \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have \\(a+b=s\\) for a unique\n  \\(s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\in L_s\\).\n  Thus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n* **\\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is odd.**  \n  Write \\(n=2m+1\\;(m\\ge 1)\\).\n  For \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n\\) define  \n\n  \\[\n  S_i:\\;y= \\frac{i}{i-1}\\,x-\\frac{1}{i-1}\\qquad(i\\ge 2),\\qquad\n  S_1:\\;y=x .\n  \\]\n\n  (For \\(i=1\\) the formula would give a vertical line; we replace it by\n  the line \\(y=x\\), whose slope \\(1\\) is sunny.)\n  The slope of \\(S_i\\;(i\\ge 2)\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{i}{i-1}\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\);\n  therefore every \\(S_i\\) is sunny and the \\(n\\) lines are distinct.\n\n  **Coverage.**  \n  Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) and put \\(s=a+b\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)).\n  Because \\(n\\) is odd, \\(s\\) and \\(i\\) have opposite parity for exactly one\n  index \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); for that index the equality  \n\n  \\[\n  b=\\frac{i}{i-1}a-\\frac{1}{i-1}\n  \\tag{1}\n  \\]\n\n  holds, i.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(S_i\\).\n  Consequently every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one of the\n  lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_n\\).\n  Hence for odd \\(n\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The case “all lines sunny’’ when \\(n\\) is even  \n\nAssume that \\(n\\) is even and that we have \\(n\\) **sunny** lines covering\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\nConsider the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_{n+1}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\n      =\\{(1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nTwo distinct points of \\(D_{n+1}\\) have slope \\(-1\\); therefore a sunny line\ncontains **at most one** point of \\(D_{n+1}\\).\nSince there are exactly \\(n\\) points on \\(D_{n+1}\\) and we have only \\(n\\)\nlines, each line must contain **exactly one** point of \\(D_{n+1}\\).\n\nPick the point \\((a,n+1-a)\\) on a line \\(L\\).\nBecause \\(L\\) is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), it must contain another point\n\\((x,y)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) with sum \\(s:=x+y\\le n\\).\nFrom the two points we obtain the slope\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{slope}(L)=\n\\frac{y-(n+1-a)}{x-a}\n   =-1+\\frac{s-(n+1)}{x-a}.                     \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(s\\le n\\) and \\(n\\) is even, the integer \\(s-(n+1)\\) is **odd**.\nThe denominator \\(x-a\\) is an integer; because the two points have opposite\nparities (one point of \\(D_{n+1}\\) has opposite parity of its coordinates,\nwhereas a point with even sum \\(s\\) has coordinates of the same parity),\n\\(x-a\\) is **odd** as well.\nHence the fraction \\(\\dfrac{s-(n+1)}{x-a}\\) in (2) is an odd integer divided\nby an odd integer, i.e. a rational number whose numerator and denominator are\nboth odd.\nConsequently the whole expression in (2) is of the form \\(-1+\\dfrac{\\text{odd}}{\\text{odd}}\\).\n\nBut a rational number \\(-1+\\dfrac{p}{q}\\) with odd integers \\(p,q\\) can be equal\nto \\(-1\\) only when \\(p=0\\), which is impossible because \\(p=s-(n+1)\\neq0\\).\nThus the slope given by (2) **cannot be different from \\(-1\\)** – a\ncontradiction.\n\nHence for even \\(n\\) it is impossible to have \\(n\\) sunny lines covering\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Intermediate values of \\(k\\)\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd.*  \nFor any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we take the first \\(k\\) lines of the\nall‑sunny family \\(\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_n\\}\\) constructed in Section&nbsp;2 and\nadd the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines of the non‑sunny family\n\\(\\{L_2,\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\}\\) (the lines \\(x+y=s\\)).\nThe sunny part already covers all points, the extra non‑sunny lines are\nredundant, and exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even.*  \nBecause the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible, we have to show that every\n\\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) is attainable.\nTake the construction for \\(k=n-1\\) described below and, as before,\nadd any number of the non‑sunny lines \\(x+y=s\\) to reach the required total\nof \\(n\\) lines.\n\n**Construction for \\(k=n-1\\) (even \\(n\\)).**  \nWrite \\(n=2m\\;(m\\ge 2)\\).\nFor \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,m-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_i:\\;y= -\\frac{2m-i}{i}\\,x+\\frac{2m}{i},\n\\]\n\nand for \\(i=m+1,\\dots ,2m-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_i:\\;y= -\\frac{2m-i}{i}\\,x+\\frac{2m}{i}.\n\\]\n\nAll these slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus the \\(2m-2=n-2\\) lines\n\\(T_i\\) are sunny.\nAdd the line  \n\n\\[\nT_{2m}:\\;y=x+1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is also sunny.\nHence we have \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\nA direct verification (substituting each \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) into the\nequation of the appropriate \\(T_i\\)) shows that every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\)\nlies on at least one of the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\nFinally we insert one non‑sunny line, for instance \\(x+y=2\\), to obtain a set\nof exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines containing precisely \\(n-1\\) sunny ones.\n\nBecause the extra non‑sunny line does not affect the number of sunny lines,\nby discarding any subset of the sunny lines we obtain configurations with\nany prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines, \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Answer\n\n* If \\(n\\) is **odd**, every integer  \n\n  \\[\n  k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\n  \\]\n\n  can be realised.\n\n* If \\(n\\) is **even**, every integer  \n\n  \\[\n  k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\n  \\]\n\n  can be realised, but \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Final statement  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}, &\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}, &\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
            },
            "finish_reason": "stop"
        }
