\\le n\\), (6) yields  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,n\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus no configuration can exist for any other value of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions for the three admissible values\n\n#### (i)  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n .\n\\]\n\nEach of them is non\u2011sunny, they are distinct, and for any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}_n\\)\nwe have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\).\nHence all points are covered and exactly \\(0\\) lines are sunny.\n\n#### (ii)  \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1 .\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover all points whose first coordinate is at most \\(n-1\\).\nThe only point of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) not yet covered is \\((n,1)\\).\nThe line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y-1=2\\,(x-n)\n\\]\n\npasses through \\((n,1)\\) and has slope \\(2\\); therefore \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.\nNow we have exactly one sunny line and all required points are covered.\n\n#### (iii)  \\(k=n\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = i\\,x + (n+1-i).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(i\\); consequently \\(L_i\\) is sunny (it is neither\nhorizontal, vertical nor of slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}_n\\).  Because \\(a\\ge1\\) we can rewrite the equation\n\\(b = i a + (n+1-i)\\) as  \n\n\\[\ni = \\frac{b-(n+1)}{a-1}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe right\u2011hand side of (9) is an integer: the numerator and denominator are\nboth integers, and the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) guarantees that the fraction\nlies between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\).  Hence there exists a unique \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)\nsatisfying (9), and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the corresponding line \\(L_i\\).\nThus the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) cover every point of\n\\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof\n\nThe inequality (7) shows that no configuration can exist for any\n\\(k\\notin\\{0,1,n\\}\\).  The explicit constructions above demonstrate that the\nthree values \\(k=0\\), \\(k=1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are indeed attainable.\nTherefore the set of all admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,n\\}} .\n\\]"
