"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad \n   N=|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   A line is *non\u2011sunny* if it is horizontal \\((y=\\text{const})\\), vertical \\((x=\\text{const})\\) or of slope \\(-1\\) \\((x+y=\\text{const})\\); otherwise it is *sunny*.\n\n2. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   *Lemma 1.* If a configuration contains a horizontal non\u2011sunny line, then **no** sunny line can appear; consequently \\(k=0\\).  \n   *Proof.* A horizontal line covers an entire row of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n   In any other row the longest possible number of points is \\(n\\).  \n   Each sunny line contributes at most one point to a given row, and each remaining non\u2011sunny line (vertical or slope \\(-1\\)) also contributes at most one point to that row.  \n   Hence, after using one horizontal line, at most \\(k+(n-k-1)=n-1\\) points can be covered in a row, which is insufficient for the top row that contains \\(n\\) points. \u220e  \n\n   *Lemma 2.* If a configuration contains a diagonal non\u2011sunny line (\\(x+y=c\\)), then again \\(k\\le 1\\).  \n   *Proof.* A diagonal line meets each column in at most one point.  \n   As in Lemma\u202f1, after fixing one diagonal line the total number of points that can be supplied to the left\u2011most column is at most \\(k+(n-k-1)=n-1\\), while that column contains \\(n\\) points. \u220e  \n\n   Consequently, **any configuration with \\(k\\ge 1\\) must contain no horizontal line and no diagonal line**; all non\u2011sunny lines, if any, are vertical.\n\n3. **Column counting argument.**  \n   Assume \\(k\\ge 2\\) and that the only non\u2011sunny lines are vertical.  \n   Let \\(v=n-k\\) be the number of vertical lines; they occupy \\(v\\) distinct columns.  \n   The remaining \\(k\\) columns contain respectively  \n\n   \\[\n   n,\\;n-1,\\;n-2,\\dots ,n-k+1\n   \\]\n\n   points (the leftmost column has \\(n\\) points, the next one \\(n-1\\), etc.).  \n   A sunny line is not vertical, therefore it meets each column in **at most one** point.  \n   Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines can supply at most \\(k\\) points to any given column.  \n\n   For the leftmost uncovered column we need \\(n\\) points, but \\(k\\le n-1\\) gives \\(k<n\\); therefore the leftmost column cannot be covered.  \n   The only way to avoid this contradiction is to have **at most one** column without a vertical line, i.e. \\(k\\le 1\\).\n\n   Thus **\\(k\\ge 2\\) is impossible**.\n\n4. **Existence for the admissible values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n .\n   \\]\n\n   They are all non\u2011sunny and each point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   They cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) except \\((n,1)\\).  \n   The line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\; y-1=2\\,(x-n)\n   \\]\n\n   passes through \\((n,1)\\) and has slope \\(2\\); therefore \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.  \n   The set consisting of the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines together with \\(\\ell\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines and contains precisely one sunny line.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f1 and Lemma\u202f2 eliminate all configurations containing a horizontal or diagonal non\u2011sunny line when \\(k\\ge 1\\).  \n   The column counting argument shows that, in the remaining case (only vertical non\u2011sunny lines), the number of sunny lines cannot exceed one.  \n   The explicit constructions above show that the two remaining possibilities \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are indeed attainable.  \n\n   Hence the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1\\}}\n\\]"

