\((x=\\text{const})\\) or of slope \\(-1\\) \\((x+y=\\text{const})\\); otherwise it is *sunny*.\n\n2. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   *Lemma 1.* If a configuration contains a horizontal non‑sunny line, then **no** sunny line can appear; consequently \\(k=0\\).  \n   *Proof.* A horizontal line covers an entire row of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n   In any other row the longest possible number of points is \\(n\\).  \n   Each sunny line contributes at most one point to a given row, and each remaining non‑sunny line (vertical or slope \\(-1\\)) also contributes at most one point to that row.  \n   Hence, after using one horizontal line, at most \\(k+(n-k-1)=n-1\\) points can be covered in a row, which is insufficient for the top row that contains \\(n\\) points. ∎  \n\n   *Lemma 2.* If a configuration contains a diagonal non‑sunny line (\\(x+y=c\\)), then again \\(k\\le 1\\).  \n   *Proof.* A diagonal line meets each column in at most one point.  \n   As in Lemma 1, after fixing one diagonal line the total number of points that can be supplied to the left‑most column is at most \\(k+(n-k-1)=n-1\\), while that column contains \\(n\\) points. ∎  \n\n   Consequently, **any configuration with \\(k\\ge 1\\) must contain no horizontal line and no diagonal line**; all non‑sunny lines, if any, are vertical.\n\n3. **Column counting argument.**  \n   Assume \\(k\\ge 2\\) and that the only non‑sunny lines are vertical.  \n   Let \\(v=n-k\\) be the number of vertical lines; they occupy \\(v\\) distinct columns.  \n   The remaining \\(k\\) columns contain respectively  \n\n   \\[\n   n,\\;n-1,\\;n-2,\\dots ,n-k+1\n   \\]\n\n   points (the leftmost column has \\(n\\) points, the next one \\(n-1\\), etc.).  \n   A sunny line is not vertical, therefore it meets each column in **at most one** point.  \n   Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines can supply at most \\(k\\) points to any given column.  \n\n   For the leftmost uncovered column we need \\(n\\) points, but \\(k\\le n-1