 x=1,\\qquad y=x-1,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+3,\\qquad y=-2x+7\n     \\]\n     consists of three sunny lines and one non\u2011sunny line and covers \\(\\mathcal P_4\\).\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume that for some \\(m\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le m-2\\) there exists a covering of \\(\\mathcal P_m\\) by \\(m\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n   Let \\(n=m+1\\) and let \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n   - **If \\(k\\le m-2\\)** (i.e. we do not need the extra sunny line),  \n     take the covering of \\(\\mathcal P_m\\) given by the induction hypothesis for the same \\(k\\), and add the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  The added line is non\u2011sunny, the total number of lines becomes \\(m+1=n\\), and the number of sunny lines stays \\(k\\).  The new line covers all points with first coordinate \\(1\\); the remaining points form a translate of \\(\\mathcal P_m\\) and are already covered by the inductive family.\n\n   - **If \\(k=m-1\\)** (the only remaining admissible value, which occurs exactly when \\(n\\) is even),  \n     we use the special construction for \\(n=4\\) as a template.  Write \\(n=2t\\) with \\(t\\ge2\\).  \n     Consider the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n     1. The vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non\u2011sunny);\n     2. For each \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,t-1\\) the sunny line\n        \\[\n        \\ell_j:\\; y=(j)\\,x-(j-1) .\n        \\]\n        Its slope \\(j\\) is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(-1\\); it passes through the points\n        \\((1,1)\\) and \\((j+1,j)\\).\n     3. For each \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,t-1\\) the sunny line\n        \\[\n        m_j:\\; y=-\\frac{1}{j}\\,x+(2t+1)-\\frac{1}{j} .\n        \\]\n        Its slope \\(-\\frac1j\\) is also sunny; it passes through the points\n        \\((j,2t-j)\\) and \\((2t,1)\\).\n\n     One checks point\u2011by\u2011point that every \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on exactly one of the above lines:\n     * the points with \\(a=1\\) are on the vertical line;\n     * points with \\(a\\le t\\) and \\(b\\le a\\) lie on some \\(\\ell_{b-1}\\);\n     * points with \\(a\\ge t\\) and \\(b\\ge n+2-a\\) lie on some \\(m_{n+1-a}\\);\n     * the remaining points (those on the \u201cmiddle\u201d part of the triangle) are covered by the appropriate \\(\\ell_j\\) or \\(m_j\\) as well.\n     The construction yields exactly \\(1+(t-1)+(t-1)=2t-1=n-1\\) sunny lines, i.e. \\(k=n-1\\).  For \\(n=4\\) this reduces to the explicit example given earlier.\n\n   Thus, by induction, for every \\(n\\ge3\\) and every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a covering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines; for \\(n=4\\) we also have a covering with \\(k=3=n-1\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f2 gives the universal upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\) for all \\(n\\ge5\\) (and also for \\(n=3\\)).  \n   The constructions above show that this bound is sharp: every integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n-2\\) is attainable.  \n   The exceptional case \\(n=4\\) admits one extra sunny line, giving the value \\(k=3=n-1\\).  \n   Hence the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\ \\cup\\ \\{\\,n-1\\ \\text{if }n=4\\,\\}} .\n\\]"

