= -2: (3,1),(4,2)\nc = -1: (2,1),(3,2),(4,3)\nc = 0: (2,2),(3,3)\nc = 1: (2,3),(3,4)\nc = 2: (2,4)\nc = 3: (2,5)\n\nSo lines with slope 1 can cover at most 3 points (c=-1 line has 3 points). So we could use line y = x - 1 (c=-1) covering (2,1),(3,2),(4,3). That's 3 points.\n\nSimilarly, line y = x - 2 (c=-2) covers (3,1),(4,2) (2 points). y = x - 3 covers (4,1),(5,2) (2 points). y = x - 4 covers (5,1) (1 point). y = x - 5 covers (6,1) (1 point). y = x (c=0) covers (2,2),(3,3) (2 points). y = x + 1 (c=1) covers (2,3),(3,4) (2 points). y = x + 2 (c=2) covers (2,4) (1 point). y = x + 3 (c=3) covers (2,5) (1 point). So we have many lines.\n\nBut we need exactly 5 sunny lines. We can try to use lines with slope 1 for some groups, and maybe other slopes for others.\n\nAlternatively, we can use lines with slope 2, -2, 1/2, -1/2, etc. Let's try to find a systematic way.\n\nWe need to cover all points. Let's try to use lines with slope 1 for groups: we have 15 points. If we use line y = x - 1 (covers 3 points). Then we need to cover remaining 12 points with 4 lines, each covering at most 3 points. So each must cover exactly 3 points. Let's see if we can partition the remaining points into 4 groups of 3 points each lying on a sunny line.\n\nRemaining points after using y = x - 1: remove (2,1),(3,2),(4,3). The remaining points:\n\n(2,2),(2,3),(2,4),(2,5)\n(3,1),(3,3),(3,4)\n(4,1),(4,2)\n(5,1),(5,2)\n(6,1)\n\nThat's 12 points.\n\nNow we need to find 4 lines covering these 12 points, each line covering exactly 3 points. Let's try to find lines with slope 2: y = 2x + c. For integer points, we need y - 2x = c constant. Compute y - 2x for each remaining point:\n\n(2,2): 2 - 4 = -2\n(2,3): 3 - 4 = -1\n(2,4): 4 - 4 = 0\n(2,5): 5 - 4 = 1\n\n(3,1): 1 - 6 = -5\n(3,3): 3 - 6 = -3\n(3,4): 4 - 6 = -2\n\n(4,1): 1 - 8 = -7\n(4,2): 2 - 8 = -6\n\n(5,1): 1 - 10 = -9\n(5,2): 2 - 10 = -8\n\n(6,1): 1 - 12 = -11\n\nSo lines with slope 2 can have at most 2 points? Let's see: c = -2 appears for (2,2) and (3,4). That's 2 points. c = -1 appears for (2,3) only. c = 0 appears for (2,4) only. c = 1 appears for (2,5) only. c = -5 appears for (3,1) only. c = -3 appears for (3,3) only. c = -7 appears for (4,1) only. c = -6 appears for (4,2) only. c = -9 appears for (5,1) only. c = -8 appears for (5,2) only. c = -11 appears for (6,1) only. So slope 2 lines cannot cover 3 points.\n\nTry slope -2: y = -2x + c. Compute y + 2x = c:\n\n(2,2): 2 + 4 = 6\n(2,3): 3 + 4 = 7\n(2,4): 4 + 4 = 8\n(2,5): 5 + 4 = 9\n\n(3,1): 1 + 6 = 7\n(3,3): 3 + 6 = 9\n(3,4): 4 + 6 = 10\n\n(4,1): 1 + 8 = 9\n(4,2): 2 + 8 = 10\n\n(5,1): 1 + 10 = 11\n(5,2): 2 + 10 = 12\n\n(6,1): 1 + 12 = 13\n\nNow group by c:\n\nc=6: (2,2)\nc=7: (2,3),(3,1) -> 2 points\nc=8: (2,4)\nc=9: (2,5),(3,3),(4,1) -> 3 points! So line y = -2x + 9 passes through (2,5),(3,3),(4,1). That's 3 points.\n\nc=10: (3,4),(4,2) -> 2 points\nc=11: (5,1)\nc=12: (5,2)\nc=13: (6,1)\n\nSo we have a line y = -2x + 9 covering (2,5),(3,3),(4,1). That's 3 points.\n\nNow we have used lines: y = x - 1 (covers (2,1),(3,2),(4,3)), and y = -2x + 9 (covers (2,5),(3,3),(4,1)). Remove those points from remaining set.\n\nRemaining points after removing those:\n\n(2,2),(2,3),(2,4)\n(3,1),(3,4)\n(4,2)\n(5,1),(5,2)\n(6,1)\n\nThat's 9 points left, need 3 lines each covering 3 points.\n\nLet's try slope -1/2: y = -0.5 x + c. Compute 2y + x = 2c? Actually for slope -1/2, line equation: y = -x/2 + c => 2y = -x + 2c => x + 2y = 2c. So x + 2y constant.\n\nCompute x + 2y for each remaining point:\n\n(2,2): 2 + 4 = 6\n(2,3): 2 + 6 = 8\n(2,4): 2 + 8 = 10\n\n(3,1): 3 + 2 = 5\n(3,4): 3 + 8 = 11\n\n(4,2): 4 + 4 = 8\n\n(5,1): 5 + 2 = 7\n(5,2): 5 + 4 = 9\n\n(6,1): 6 + 2 = 8\n\nGroup by x+2y:\n\n5: (3,1)\n6: (2,2)\n7: (5,1)\n8: (2,3),(4,2),(6,1) -> 3 points! So line x + 2y = 8 => y = (8 - x)/2 = -x/2 + 4. So line y = -0.5x + 4 passes through (2,3),(4,2),(6,1). That's 3 points.\n\n9: (5,2)\n10: (2,4)\n11: (3,4)\n\nNow we have used line x+2y=8 covering (2,3),(4,2),(6,1). Remove those.\n\nRemaining points:\n\n(2,2),(2,4)\n(3,1),(3,4)\n(5,1),(5,2)\n\nThat's 6 points left, need 2 lines each covering 3 points.\n\nLet's try slope 2 again? Already tried. Let's try slope 1/2: y = 0.5 x + c => 2y = x + 2c => x - 2y = constant? Actually 2y - x = constant. Compute 2y - x for each remaining point:\n\n(2,2): 4 - 2 = 2\n(2,4): 8 - 2 = 6\n\n(3,1): 2 - 3 = -1\n(3,4): 8 - 3 = 5\n\n(5,1): 2 - 5 = -3\n(5,2): 4 - 5 = -1\n\nGroup:\n\n-3: (5,1)\n-1: (3,1),(5,2) -> 2 points\n2: (2,2)\n5: (3,4)\n6: (2,4)\n\nSo slope 1/2 lines cannot cover 3 points.\n\nTry slope -1/3: y = -x/3 + c => 3y = -x + 3c => x + 3y = constant. Compute x + 3y:\n\n(2,2): 2 + 6 = 8\n(2,4): 2 + 12 = 14\n\n(3,1): 3 + 3 = 6\n(3,4): 3 + 12 = 15\n\n(5,1): 5 + 3 = 8\n(5,2): 5 + 6 = 11\n\nGroup:\n\n6: (3,1)\n8: (2,2),(5,1) -> 2 points\n11: (5,2)\n14: (2,4)\n15: (3,4)\n\nSo not 3.\n\nTry slope 2/3: y = (2/3)x + c => 3y = 2x + 3c => 3y - 2x = constant. Compute 3y - 2x:\n\n(2,2): 6 - 4 = 2\n(2,4): 12 - 4 = 8\n\n(3,1): 3 - 6 = -3\n(3,4): 12 - 6 = 6\n\n(5,1): 3 - 10 = -7\n(5,2): 6 - 10 = -4\n\nGroup:\n\n-7: (5,1)\n-4: (5,2)\n-3: (3,1)\n2: (2,2)\n6: (3,4)\n8: (2,4)\n\nNo 3.\n\nTry slope -3/2: y = -1.5x + c => 2y = -3x + 2c => 3x + 2y = constant. Compute 3x + 2y:\n\n(2,2): 6 + 4 = 10\n(2,4): 6 + 8 = 14\n\n(3,1): 9 + 2 = 11\n(3,4): 9 + 8 = 17\n\n(5,1): 15 + 2 = 17\n(5,2): 15 + 4 = 19\n\nGroup:\n\n10: (2,2)\n11: (3,1)\n14: (2,4)\n17: (3,4),(5,1) -> 2 points\n19: (5,2)\n\nNo 3.\n\nTry slope 3: y = 3x + c => y - 3x = c. Compute y - 3x:\n\n(2,2): 2 - 6 = -4\n(2,4): 4 - 6 = -2\n\n(3,1): 1 - 9 = -8\n(3,4): 4 - 9 = -5\n\n(5,1): 1 - 15 = -14\n(5,2): 2 - 15 = -13\n\nGroup: all distinct.\n\nTry slope -3: y = -3x + c => y + 3x = c. Compute y + 3x:\n\n(2,2): 2 + 6 = 8\n(2,4): 4 + 6 = 10\n\n(3,1): 1 + 9 = 10\n(3,4): 4 + 9 = 13\n\n(5,1): 1 + 15 = 16\n(5,2): 2 + 15 = 17\n\nGroup: 8: (2,2); 10: (2,4),(3,1); 13: (3,4); 16: (5,1); 17: (5,2). So line y = -3x + 10 passes through (2,4) and (3,1) (2 points). Not 3.\n\nTry slope 1/3: y = x/3 + c => 3y = x + 3c => x - 3y = constant. Compute x - 3y:\n\n(2,2): 2 - 6 = -4\n(2,4): 2 - 12 = -10\n\n(3,1): 3 - 3 = 0\n(3,4): 3 - 12 = -9\n\n(5,1): 5 - 3 = 2\n(5,2): 5 - 6 = -1\n\nGroup: all distinct.\n\nTry slope -1/4: y = -x/4 + c => 4y = -x + 4c => x + 4y = constant. Compute x + 4y:\n\n(2,2): 2 + 8 = 10\n(2,4): 2 + 16 = 18\n\n(3,1): 3 + 4 = 7\n(3,4): 3 + 16 = 19\n\n(5,1): 5 + 4 = 9\n(5,2): 5 + 8 = 13\n\nAll distinct.\n\nTry slope 2/5: y = (2/5)x + c => 5y = 2x + 5c => 5y - 2x = constant. Compute 5y - 2x:\n\n(2,2): 10 - 4 = 6\n(2,4): 20 - 4 = 16\n\n(3,1): 5 - 6 = -1\n(3,4): 20 - 6 = 14\n\n(5,1): 5 - 10 = -5\n(5,2): 10 - 10 = 0\n\nAll distinct.\n\nTry slope -2/5: y = -2x/5 + c => 5y = -2x + 5c => 2x + 5y = constant. Compute 2x + 5y:\n\n(2,2): 4 + 10 = 14\n(2,4): 4 + 20 = 24\n\n(3,1): 6 + 5 = 11\n(3,4): 6 + 20 = 26\n\n(5,1): 10 + 5 = 15\n(5,2): 10 + 10 = 20\n\nAll distinct.\n\nThus we haven't found a line covering 3 points among the remaining 6 points. Let's try to see if we can group them differently earlier. Perhaps we need to choose different initial lines.\n\nWe used y = x - 1 and y = -2x + 9 and y = x + 2y = 8. Maybe we can find a different set of 5 lines covering all points. Let's try a systematic approach: we need to partition Q into 5 lines each covering 3 points. Since each line can have at most 3 points, we need each line to have exactly 3 points. So we need to find 5 triples of points that lie on sunny lines.\n\nWe can try to find a set of 5 triples that partition Q. Let's list all possible triples of points that lie on a sunny line. We can search manually but it's a bit tedious. However, we can try to find a pattern: maybe we can use lines with slope 1 (c = -1) gave triple (2,1),(3,2),(4,3). Similarly, slope -2 gave triple (2,5),(3,3),(4,1). Slope -1/2 gave triple (2,3),(4,2),(6,1). That's three triples. We need two more triples covering the remaining points: (2,2),(2,4),(3,1),(3,4),(5,1),(5,2). That's 6 points left. We need two triples covering these. Let's see if we can find a line covering (2,2),(3,4),(5,? maybe). Let's compute slope between (2,2) and (3,4): slope = (4-2)/(3-2) = 2. So line y = 2x + c: plugging (2,2) gives 2 = 4 + c => c = -2. So line y = 2x - 2 passes through (2,2) and (3,4). Does it also pass through (5,? )? For x=5, y = 2*5 - 2 = 8, not in Q. So not triple.\n\nCheck line through (2,2) and (5,1): slope = (1-2)/(5-2) = -1/3. So line y = -1/3 x + c. Plug (2,2): 2 = -2/3 + c => c = 2 + 2/3 = 8/3. So line y = -x/3 + 8/3. Does it pass through (3,? )? For x=3, y = -1 + 8/3 = 5/3, not integer. So not triple.\n\nCheck line through (2,2) and (5,2): slope = (2-2)/(5-2) = 0, horizontal, which is non-sunny. So not allowed.\n\nCheck line through (2,4) and (3,1): slope = (1-4)/(3-2) = -3. So line y = -3x + c. Plug (2,4): 4 = -6 + c => c = 10. So line y = -3x + 10 passes through (2,4) and (3,1). Does it also pass through (5,? )? For x=5, y = -15 + 10 = -5, not in Q. So not triple.\n\nCheck line through (2,4) and (5,1): slope = (1-4)/(5-2) = -3/3 = -1, which is non-sunny. So not allowed.\n\nCheck line through (2,4) and (5,2): slope = (2-4)/(5-2) = -2/3. So line y = -2/3 x + c. Plug (2,4): 4 = -4/3 + c => c = 4 + 4/3 = 16/3. So line y = -2/3 x + 16/3. Does it pass through (3,? )? For x=3, y = -2 + 16/3 = ( -6 + 16)/3 = 10/3, not integer. So not triple.\n\nCheck line through (3,1) and (5,2): slope = (2-1)/(5-3) = 1/2. So line y = 0.5 x + c. Plug (3,1): 1 = 1.5 + c => c = -0.5. So line y = 0.5 x - 0.5. Does it pass through (2,? )? For x=2, y = 1 - 0.5 = 0.5, not integer. So not triple.\n\nCheck line through (3,1) and (5,1): slope = (1-1)/(5-3) = 0, horizontal, non-sunny.\n\nCheck line through (3,4) and (5,1): slope = (1-4)/(5-3) = -3/2 = -1.5, which is not -1, so sunny. So line y = -1.5 x + c. Plug (3,4): 4 = -4.5 + c => c = 8.5. So line y = -1.5 x + 8.5. Does it pass through (2,? )? For x=2, y = -3 + 8.5 = 5.5, not integer. So not triple.\n\nCheck line through (3,4) and (5,2): slope = (2-4)/(5-3) = -2/2 = -1, which is non-sunny.\n\nCheck line through (2,2) and (3,1): slope = (1-2)/(3-2) = -1, non-sunny.\n\nCheck line through (2,2) and (5,1): slope = -1/3, we already considered.\n\nCheck line through (2,2) and (3,4): slope = 2, we considered.\n\nCheck line through (2,2) and (5,2): slope = 0, non-sunny.\n\nCheck line through (2,4) and (3,4): slope = 0, non-sunny.\n\nCheck line through (2,4) and (5,2): slope = -2/3, considered.\n\nCheck line through (2,4) and (3,1): slope = -3, considered.\n\nCheck line through (2,4) and (5,1): slope = -1, non-sunny.\n\nCheck line through (2,2) and (3,1): slope = -1, non-sunny.\n\nCheck line through (2,2) and (4,2): slope = (2-2)/(4-2) = 0, non-sunny.\n\nCheck line through (2,2) and (5,2): slope = 0, non-sunny.\n\nCheck line through (2,2) and (4,1): slope = (1-2)/(4-2) = -1/2, sunny. So line y = -0.5 x + c. Plug (2,2): 2 = -1 + c => c = 3. So line y = -0.5 x + 3. Does it pass through (4,1)? For x=4, y = -2 + 3 = 1, yes. So line passes through (2,2) and (4,1). Does it also pass through (6,? )? For x=6, y = -3 + 3 = 0, not in Q. So only two points.\n\nCheck line through (2,2) and (5,1): slope = -1/3, we considered.\n\nCheck line through (2,2) and (3,4): slope = 2, we considered.\n\nCheck line through (2,2) and (5,2): slope = 0, non-sunny.\n\nCheck line through (2,2) and (3,1): slope = -1, non-sunny.\n\nCheck line through (2,2) and (4,2): slope = 0, non-sunny.\n\nCheck line through (2,2) and (5,1): slope = -1/3, considered.\n\nCheck line through (2,2) and (5,2): slope = 0, non-sunny.\n\nCheck line through (2,2) and (3,1): slope = -1, non-sunny.\n\nCheck line through (2,2) and (4,1): slope = -1/2, considered.\n\nCheck line through (2,2) and (5,1): slope = -1/3, considered.\n\nCheck line through (2,2) and (5,2): slope = 0, non-sunny.\n\nThus we haven't found a triple covering the remaining 6 points. Let's try a different initial selection of triples. Perhaps we can find a different set of 5 triples covering all points. Let's try to find a systematic approach: we need to partition Q into 5 triples. Let's list all possible triples of points that lie on a sunny line. We can try to find all lines with slope not 0,∞,-1 that pass through at least 3 points of Q. Let's search manually.\n\nWe have Q points as above. Let's compute all possible slopes between pairs and see if any line passes through a third point.\n\nWe can generate all pairs (p,q) and compute slope m = (y2 - y1)/(x2 - x1). Then check if any third point lies on same line.\n\nBetter approach: Since Q is small (15 points), we can try to find all lines with at least 3 points. Let's list points with coordinates:\n\nList them again:\n\n(2,1)\n(2,2)\n(2,3)\n(2,4)\n(2,5)\n(3,1)\n(3,2)\n(3,3)\n(3,4)\n(4,1)\n(4,2)\n(4,3)\n(5,1)\n(5,2)\n(6,1)\n\nWe need to find lines with slope not 0,∞,-1 that contain at least 3 points.\n\nWe already found:\n\n- Slope 1: line y = x - 1 passes through (2,1),(3,2),(4,3). That's triple.\n\n- Slope -2: line y = -2x + 9 passes through (2,5),(3,3),(4,1). That's triple.\n\n- Slope -1/2: line y = -0.5x + 4 passes through (2,3),(4,2),(6,1). That's triple.\n\n- Slope -1/2 also passes through (3,2.5)? Not integer.\n\n- Slope -1/2 also passes through (5,? )? For x=5, y = -2.5 + 4 = 1.5, not integer.\n\n- Slope -1/2 also passes through (3,2.5) not integer.\n\n- Slope -1/2 also passes through (1,4.5) not in Q.\n\n- Slope -1/2 also passes through (2,3) etc.\n\n- Slope -1/2 also passes through (4,2) and (6,1) as we have.\n\n- Slope -1/2 also passes through (5,? ) not integer.\n\n- Slope -1/2 also passes through (3,2.5) not integer.\n\nThus we have three triples.\n\nNow we need to find two more triples covering the remaining points:\n\nRemaining points after removing those triples:\n\nRemove (2,1),(3,2),(4,3) from slope 1 triple.\n\nRemove (2,5),(3,3),(4,1) from slope -2 triple.\n\nRemove (2,3),(4,2),(6,1) from slope -1/2 triple.\n\nRemaining points:\n\n(2,2)\n(2,4)\n(3,1)\n(3,4)\n(5,1)\n(5,2)\n\nThat's exactly the 6 points we had earlier. So we need to partition these into two triples.\n\nNow we need to find lines (sunny) that contain three of these points each.\n\nLet's examine possible triples among these six points.\n\nList them:\n\nA: (2,2)\nB: (2,4)\nC: (3,1)\nD: (3,4)\nE: (5,1)\nF: (5,2)\n\nWe need to find lines with slope not 0,∞,-1 that contain three of these.\n\nCheck if any three are collinear.\n\nFirst, check if (2,2),(3,4),(5,? ) maybe? Let's compute slope between (2,2) and (3,4): slope = (4-2)/(3-2) = 2. So line y = 2x + c, c = -2. So line y = 2x - 2. Does it pass through (5,? )? For x=5, y = 10 - 2 = 8, not in set. So not triple.\n\nCheck (2,2),(5,2): slope = (2-2)/(5-2) = 0, non-sunny.\n\nCheck (2,2),(5,1): slope = (1-2)/(5-2) = -1/3, sunny. Equation: y = -x/3 + c. Plug (2,2): 2 = -2/3 + c => c = 2 + 2/3 = 8/3. So line y = -x/3 + 8/3. Does it pass through (3,? )? For x=3, y = -1 + 8/3 = 5/3, not integer. So not triple.\n\nCheck (2,2),(3,1): slope = (1-2)/(3-2) = -1, non-sunny.\n\nCheck (2,2),(3,4): slope = 2, we did.\n\nCheck (2,2),(5,? ) we did.\n\nCheck (2,2),(3,? ) we did.\n\nCheck (2,2),(4,? ) not in set.\n\nCheck (2,4),(3,1): slope = (1-4)/(3-2) = -3, sunny. Equation: y = -3x + c. Plug (2,4): 4 = -6 + c => c = 10. So line y = -3x + 10. Does it pass through (5,? )? For x=5, y = -15 + 10 = -5, not in set. So not triple.\n\nCheck (2,4),(3,4): slope = 0, non-sunny.\n\nCheck (2,4),(5,1): slope = (1-4)/(5-2) = -3/3 = -1, non-sunny.\n\nCheck (2,4),(5,2): slope = (2-4)/(5-2) = -2/3, sunny. Equation: y = -2/3 x + c. Plug (2,4): 4 = -4/3 + c => c = 4 + 4/3 = 16/3. So line y = -2/3 x + 16/3. Does it pass through (3,? )? For x=3, y = -2 + 16/3 = ( -6 + 16)/3 = 10/3, not integer. So not triple.\n\nCheck (2,4),(3,1): we did.\n\nCheck (2,4),(3,4): slope 0, non-sunny.\n\nCheck (2,4),(5,1): slope -1, non-sunny.\n\nCheck (2,4),(5,2): slope -2/3, we did.\n\nCheck (3,1),(5,1): slope 0, non-sunny.\n\nCheck (3,1),(5,2): slope = (2-1)/(5-3) = 1/2, sunny. Equation: y = 0.5 x + c. Plug (3,1): 1 = 1.5 + c => c = -0.5. So line y = 0.5 x - 0.5. Does it pass through (2,? )? For x=2, y = 1 - 0.5 = 0.5, not integer. Does it pass through (4,? )? For x=4, y = 2 - 0.5 = 1.5, not integer. So not triple.\n\nCheck (3,1),(2,2): slope = (2-1)/(2-3) = 1/(-1) = -1, non-sunny.\n\nCheck (3,1),(2,4): slope = (4-1)/(2-3) = 3/(-1) = -3, sunny. Equation: y = -3x + c. Plug (3,1): 1 = -9 + c => c = 10. So line y = -3x + 10. Does it pass through (5,? )? For x=5, y = -15 + 10 = -5, not in set. So not triple.\n\nCheck (3,1),(5,2): we did.\n\nCheck (3,4),(5,1): slope = (1-4)/(5-3) = -3/2 = -1.5, sunny. Equation: y = -1.5 x + c. Plug (3,4): 4 = -4.5 + c => c = 8.5. So line y = -1.5 x + 8.5. Does it pass through (2,? )? For x=2, y = -3 + 8.5 = 5.5, not integer. So not triple.\n\nCheck (3,4),(5,2): slope = (2-4)/(5-3) = -2/2 = -1, non-sunny.\n\nCheck (3,4),(2,2): slope = (2-4)/(2-3) = -2/(-1) = 2, sunny. Equation: y = 2x + c. Plug (3,4): 4 = 6 + c => c = -2. So line y = 2x - 2. Does it pass through (5,? )? For x=5, y = 10 - 2 = 8, not in set. So not triple.\n\nCheck (3,4),(2,4): slope = 0, non-sunny.\n\nCheck (3,4),(2,? ) we did.\n\nCheck (5,1),(5,2): vertical line x=5, non-sunny.\n\nCheck (5,1),(2,2): slope = (2-1)/(2-5) = 1/(-3) = -1/3, sunny. Equation: y = -x/3 + c. Plug (5,1): 1 = -5/3 + c => c = 1 + 5/3 = 8/3. So line y = -x/3 + 8/3. Does it pass through (2,2)? For x=2, y = -2/3 + 8/3 = 6/3 = 2, yes! So line passes through (5,1) and (2,2). Does it also pass through (8,? )? Not needed. So we have a line with points (2,2) and (5,1). That's only two points. Need a third point among remaining set: (3,? ), (4,? ), (5,2), (2,4), (3,1), (3,4). Check if any of those also lie on this line: For x=3, y = -1 + 8/3 = 5/3, not integer. For x=4, y = -4/3 + 8/3 = 4/3, not integer. For x=5, we have (5,1) already. So no third point.\n\nCheck (5,2),(2,4): slope = (4-2)/(2-5) = 2/(-3) = -2/3, sunny. Equation: y = -2/3 x + c. Plug (5,2): 2 = -10/3 + c => c = 2 + 10/3 = 16/3. So line y = -2/3 x + 16/3