 remaining set with exactly \\(n-3\\) sunny lines.  \n   Thus we obtain a configuration with \\(k=n-3\\) sunny lines.  \n   Deleting any sunny line and inserting a non\u2011sunny one reduces \\(k\\) by one, so every integer \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\) is realizable.\n\n8. **Collecting the results.**  \n   Combining the lower bounds (\\(k\\ge0\\)), the universal upper bounds (5) and (6), and the explicit constructions, we obtain precisely the sets of admissible \\(k\\) listed in the statement of the theorem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A trivial family (all non\u2011sunny)\n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on exactly one of them.  \nHence  \n\n\\[\nk=0\\quad\\text{is always attainable.} \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Parity property of sunny lines  \n\nWrite a sunny line in reduced rational form  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac pq\\,x+r,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1,\\;p\\notin\\{0,-q\\}.\n\\]\n\nTwo consecutive integer points on the line differ by the vector \\((q,-p)\\); therefore the sum \\(a+b\\) changes by  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta = q-p .\n\\]\n\nBecause the line is sunny, \\(p\\neq0\\) and \\(p\\neq -q\\); consequently \\(|\\Delta|\\ge2\\).  \nThus all integer points of a sunny line have the **same colour** in the checkerboard colouring (black if \\(a+b\\) even, white otherwise).  \n\nInside the triangular set \\(S_m\\) a fixed colour occurs at most  \n\n\\[\nM_m:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{m+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\ntimes on any sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Impossibility of covering \\(S_m\\) with \\(m\\) sunny lines for \\(m\\ge4\\)\n\nColour the lattice points black when \\(a+b\\) is even and white otherwise.  \nIn \\(S_m\\) the numbers of black and white points are  \n\n\\[\nB_m=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m(m+1)}4\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\\qquad\nW_m=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{m(m+1)}4\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAssume that \\(S_m\\) is covered by \\(m\\) sunny lines.  \nEach line can contain at most \\(M_m\\) black points by (2); therefore the total number of black points covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nmM_m = m\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{m+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nA direct verification gives  \n\n\\[\nB_m > m\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{m+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil \\qquad\\text{for every }m\\ge4 . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, (5) holds for \\(m=4,5,6\\) by explicit calculation, and when \\(m\\) increases by one the left\u2011hand side of (3) grows by at least \\(\\frac{m+1}{2}\\) whereas the right\u2011hand side of (4) grows by at most \\(\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{m+2}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\); thus the inequality persists for all larger \\(m\\).\n\nInequality (5) contradicts the assumption that the \\(m\\) sunny lines cover all black points of \\(S_m\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }m\\ge4,\\;S_m\\text{ cannot be covered by }m\\text{ sunny lines.}} \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  At least two non\u2011sunny lines for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\n*Lemma 7.*  For every \\(n\\ge4\\) any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) must contain at least two non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  Suppose at most one line is non\u2011sunny.  By a rotation of the plane we may assume that the possible non\u2011sunny line is the vertical line \\(x=1\\); it covers the \\(n\\) points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\).  \nThe remaining points form a copy of \\(S_{n-1}\\) and number \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\).  \nEach of the other \\(n-1\\) lines is sunny, and by (2) can contain at most \\(\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\) of those points.  Hence together they cover at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil < \\frac{n(n-1)}2 ,\n\\]\n\na contradiction. \u220e  \n\nConsequently, for all \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  A stronger bound for large \\(n\\)\n\nTake the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\).  \nThe points not covered are  \n\n\\[\nT_{n-2}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a translate of \\(S_{n-2}\\).  \n\nIf \\(n-2\\ge4\\) then, by (6), \\(S_{n-2}\\) cannot be covered by \\(n-2\\) sunny lines; therefore the two\u2011border construction fails and at least **three** non\u2011sunny lines are necessary.  Hence for \\(n\\ge6\\)\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-3 . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Constructions attaining the bounds  \n\n##### (i)  \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(S_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n\\]\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the three vertical lines \\(x=1,2,3\\).  \n* \\(k=1\\): sunny line \\(L_s:y=x\\) together with the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\).  \n* \\(k=3\\): three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\nL_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad\nL_3:\\;y=-2x+5 .\n\\]\n\nVerification (each point lies on a listed line):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{point}&\\text{covering line}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1)&L_1\\\\\n(2,2)&L_1\\\\\n(1,2)&L_2\\\\\n(3,1)&L_2\\\\\n(1,3)&L_3\\\\\n(2,1)&L_3\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAll three lines have slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence they are sunny.  \nA short exhaustive check shows that no configuration with exactly two sunny lines can cover all six points, so the admissible values are \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n##### (ii)  \\(n=4\\) and \\(n=5\\)\n\nUse the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\).  \nThe uncovered set is a translate of \\(S_{n-2}\\) with \\(n-2\\le3\\).\n\n* For \\(n=4\\) the remaining set is \\(\\{(2,2)\\}\\), covered by the sunny line \\(y=x\\).  \n* For \\(n=5\\) the remaining set is \\(S_3\\), which is covered by the three sunny lines displayed in the \\(n=3\\) case.\n\nThus we obtain configurations with exactly \\(k=n-2\\) sunny lines.  \nBy replacing sunny lines with additional non\u2011sunny ones we realise every intermediate value \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\).\n\n##### (iii)  \\(n\\ge6\\)\n\nTake the three non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\qquad y=1,\\qquad x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThey cover the first row, the first column and the outer diagonal of \\(S_n\\).  \nThe points not covered form a translate of \\(S_{n-3}\\).  \nSince \\(n-3\\le3\\) when \\(n=6,7,8\\) and for larger \\(n\\) we can apply the construction recursively, we can cover this remaining set with exactly \\(n-3\\) sunny lines (the construction for \\(S_3\\) works for any translate).  \nHence we obtain a family with \\(k=n-3\\) sunny lines.  \nRemoving any sunny line and inserting a non\u2011sunny one reduces \\(k\\) by one, so every integer \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Final enumeration  \n\nCollecting the lower bounds (\\(k\\ge0\\)), the universal upper bounds (7) and (8), and the explicit constructions, we obtain:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2pt]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}, & n=4\\text{ or }n=5,\\\\[2pt]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-3\\}, & n\\ge 6 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nThese are exactly the non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem. \u220e"
