 -2/3, -4/3, -3/4, etc. Let's systematically search for lines that contain 3 points.\n\nWe can think of the points as coordinates (a,b) with a+b ≤ 6. Let's list all points again:\n\n(1,1) sum2\n(1,2) sum3\n(1,3) sum4\n(1,4) sum5\n(1,5) sum6\n(2,1) sum3\n(2,2) sum4\n(2,3) sum5\n(2,4) sum6\n(3,1) sum4\n(3,2) sum5\n(3,3) sum6\n(4,1) sum5\n(4,2) sum6\n(5,1) sum6\n\nNow, we need to find lines of the form p x + q y = d, with p,q positive integers, gcd(p,q)=1, p≠q, and each line must contain exactly 3 points from this set.\n\nWe can try to find all such lines. Let's enumerate possible (p,q) pairs with p,q ≤ maybe 5 (since slopes with larger p,q may produce too few points). For each (p,q), we can find d such that the line passes through points in the set.\n\nWe can also think of lines with slope -p/q where p and q differ by 1 (|p-q|=1) because those give the maximum number of points. For n=5, M_n = 3, so we need lines with |p-q|=1. So p,q must be consecutive integers. So possible (p,q) pairs: (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2), (3,4), (4,3), (4,5), (5,4), etc. But p,q must be positive and gcd=1, which holds for consecutive integers. So slopes: -1/2, -2, -2/3, -3/2, -3/4, -4/3, -4/5, -5/4, etc. For each slope, the line equation is p x + q y = d. The sum x+y = (d + (q-p) x)/q. Since q-p = ±1, the sum changes by 1 each step. So the line can have up to n points, but positivity constraints limit to at most floor((n+1)/2) = 3. So each such line can have at most 3 points. So we need to find lines of these slopes that have exactly 3 points.\n\nLet's find all lines of slope -1/2 (p=1,q=2) that have 3 points. The line equation: x + 2y = d. For integer points, x must have same parity as d. The points in S_5 satisfy a+b ≤ 6. Let's find d values that give 3 points.\n\nWe need to find d such that there are 3 integer solutions (x,y) with x≥1, y≥1, x+y ≤ 6, and x+2y = d.\n\nWe can solve for y = (d - x)/2. So for each x of parity matching d, we need y integer ≥1, and x+y ≤6.\n\nLet's enumerate possible d values. Since x+2y = d, and x,y≥1, we have d ≥ 1+2*1 = 3. Also x+2y ≤ x + 2(6 - x) = 12 - x ≤ 11 (since x≥1). So d ≤ 11. So d ∈ [3,11].\n\nNow for each d, we can find solutions. Let's do it systematically.\n\nd=3: parity odd => x odd. x=1 => y = (3-1)/2 = 1 => (1,1) sum2 ≤6. x=3 => y = (3-3)/2 = 0 => y=0 invalid. So only (1,1). So 1 point.\n\nd=4: parity even => x even. x=2 => y = (4-2)/2 = 1 => (2,1) sum3 ≤6. x=4 => y = (4-4)/2 = 0 invalid. So only (2,1). So 1 point.\n\nd=5: odd => x odd. x=1 => y = (5-1)/2 = 2 => (1,2) sum3. x=3 => y = (5-3)/2 = 1 => (3,1) sum4. x=5 => y = (5-5)/2 = 0 invalid. So points: (1,2), (3,1). That's 2 points.\n\nd=6: even => x even. x=2 => y = (6-2)/2 = 2 => (2,2) sum4. x=4 => y = (6-4)/2 = 1 => (4,1) sum5. x=6 => y = (6-6)/2 = 0 invalid. So points: (2,2), (4,1). That's 2 points.\n\nd=7: odd => x odd. x=1 => y = (7-1)/2 = 3 => (1,3) sum4. x=3 => y = (7-3)/2 = 2 => (3,2) sum5. x=5 => y = (7-5)/2 = 1 => (5,1) sum6. x=7 => y = (7-7)/2 = 0 invalid. So points: (1,3), (3,2), (5,1). That's 3 points. Good.\n\nd=8: even => x even. x=2 => y = (8-2)/2 = 3 => (2,3) sum5. x=4 => y = (8-4)/2 = 2 => (4,2) sum6. x=6 => y = (8-6)/2 = 1 => (6,1) invalid because a=6 > n? Actually a can be up to n=5, but a=6 >5, so not in S_5. So only (2,3), (4,2). That's 2 points.\n\nd=9: odd => x odd. x=1 => y = (9-1)/2 = 4 => (1,4) sum5. x=3 => y = (9-3)/2 = 3 => (3,3) sum6. x=5 => y = (9-5)/2 = 2 => (5,2) sum7 >6 invalid. x=7 => y = (9-7)/2 = 1 => (7,1) invalid. So points: (1,4), (3,3). That's 2 points.\n\nd=10: even => x even. x=2 => y = (10-2)/2 = 4 => (2,4) sum6. x=4 => y = (10-4)/2 = 3 => (4,3) sum7 invalid. x=6 => y = (10-6)/2 = 2 => (6,2) invalid. So only (2,4). That's 1 point.\n\nd=11: odd => x odd. x=1 => y = (11-1)/2 = 5 => (1,5) sum6. x=3 => y = (11-3)/2 = 4 => (3,4) sum7 invalid. x=5 => y = (11-5)/2 = 3 => (5,3) sum8 invalid. So only (1,5). That's 1 point.\n\nThus the only line of slope -1/2 that contains 3 points is x+2y=7. So we have exactly one such 