rightarrow\\qquad k\\le 1 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\).**  \n   Inequality (2) proves that for any \\(n\\ge4\\) a configuration with exactly two (or more) sunny lines cannot exist.  \n\n6. **Existence for the remaining values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Use the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   They are all non‑sunny, distinct, and together contain every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Take the vertical lines \\(x=1,x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) (these are \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines).  \n   The only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\).  \n   Add the sunny line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\); its slope is \\(-\\dfrac{1}{\\,n-1\\,}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), so it is sunny.  \n   All points of \\(S_n\\) are now covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n7. **The special case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   The set \\(S_3\\) consists of six points.  \n   *\\(k=0\\):* three vertical lines \\(x=1,2,3\\).  \n   *\\(k=1\\):* the sunny line \\(y=x\\) together with the non‑sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\).  \n   *\\(k=3\\):* the three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad y=-2x+5\n   \\]\n\n   cover all six points (a complete point‑by‑point verification is given in the detailed proof).  \n   No configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists because a sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(S_3\\); two sunny lines would therefore cover at most four points, leaving at least two uncovered.\n\n8. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the lower bound (2) with the explicit constructions yields the full description of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2pt]\n\\{0,1\\},   & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]   ∎  \n\n---  \n\n**Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  How many points can a line contain?\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*  \nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets \\(S_n\\) in the points \\((c,b)\\) with  \n\\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); hence at most \\(n\\) points.  \nA horizontal line \\(y=c\\) and a line of slope \\(-1\\) (equation \\(x+y=c\\))\nhave the same bound, because the largest possible value of \\(c\\) is\n\\(n+1\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nWrite a sunny line in reduced form  \n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{p}{q}x+c ,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\; \\gcd(p,q)=1,\\;p\\neq q .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIf \\((x,y)\\in S_n\\) lies on (2) then \\(x\\equiv cq\\pmod q\\); therefore the\nabscissae of the integer points on the line form an arithmetic\nprogression with common difference \\(q\\).  Since all abscissae must lie in\n\\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\), the progression can contain at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{q}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nterms (the inequality is strict unless \\(q=1\\) or \\(q=2\\)).  \nThe bound is attained for the line of slope \\(-2\\) and intercept\n\\(c=n+2\\); its integer points are  \n\n\\[\n(1,n),\\;(2,n-2),\\;(3,n-4),\\dots ,\n\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\nn+2-2\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr),\n\\]\n\nall belonging to \\(S_n\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{M_n:=\\max\\{\\text{# points of a sunny line in }S_n\\}\n      =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor } .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A column‑counting lower bound\n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering\n\\(S_n\\) and that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  Put  \n\n\\[\ns:=n-k\\qquad(\\text{the number of non‑sunny lines}).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nConsider a fixed column \\(x=j\\) (\\(1\\le j\\le n\\)).  \nA sunny line is not vertical, hence it can meet this column in **at most\none** point.  A non‑sunny line can meet the column in more than one\npoint only when it is the vertical line \\(x=j\\); otherwise it also meets\nthe column in at most one point.  Therefore the total number of points\nof \\(S_n\\) that can be covered inside column \\(j\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\text{vertical non‑sunny lines equal to }x=j\\bigr)+k .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Forced vertical lines and the inequality \\(k\\le1\\)\n\nColumn 1 contains exactly \\(n\\) points.  In (6) the term “vertical\nnon‑sunny lines equal to \\(x=1\\)” can be either \\(0\\) or \\(1\\).  Since the\nright‑hand side must be at least \\(n\\), we must have that vertical line\npresent; i.e. \\(x=1\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\nAfter using the line \\(x=1\\) we have \\(s-1\\) non‑sunny lines left.\nColumn 2 contains \\(n-1\\) points.  Applying (6) to this column gives  \n\n\\[\n(\\text{vertical line }x=2\\text{ present?})+(s-1)+k\\ge n-1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\((s-1)+k=n-1\\), the inequality forces the presence of the vertical\nline \\(x=2\\).  Repeating the argument for columns \\(3,4,\\dots ,s\\) we\nobtain that **each of the first \\(s\\) columns must be covered by a distinct\nvertical line**.  Hence we need at least \\(s\\) vertical lines, i.e.  \n\n\\[\ns\\ge n-1\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k=n-s\\le 1 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus for every \\(n\\ge4\\) a configuration with exactly two (or more)\nsunny lines cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Existence for the admissible values\n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n\\]\n\nAll are non‑sunny, distinct, and every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies\n\\(x=a\\), so it lies on the line \\(x=a\\).\n\n*Case \\(k=1\\).*  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1)\n\\]\n\nbe the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines.  They cover all points of \\(S_n\\) except\n\\((n,1)\\).  Add the line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y=-\\frac{1}{\\,n-1\\,}x+\\frac{n+1}{\\,n-1\\,},\n\\]\n\nwhich passes through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\).  Its slope \\(-\\dfrac{1}{n-1}\\)\nis different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(L\\) is sunny.  The family  \n\n\\[\n\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-1},L\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly one sunny line and covers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  The exceptional case \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\(S_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).\n\n* \\(k=0\\): three vertical lines \\(x=1,2,3\\).  \n* \\(k=1\\): the sunny line \\(y=x\\) together with the non‑sunny lines\n  \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\).  \n* \\(k=3\\): the three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad y=-2x+5 .\n  \\]\n\n  Substituting each of the six points into the three equations shows that\n  every point satisfies at least one of them (the verification table is\n  given in the detailed proof).  \n\nA sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(S_3\\); consequently two\nsunny lines can cover at most four points, leaving at least two uncovered.\nHence \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Final answer\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2pt]\n\\{0,1\\},   & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]   ∎"
